WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.230
Hi everyone, we're done with the break.

2
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:15.190
Taking this opportunity, I'd like to introduce our next speaker, Tamara Kojić,

3
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.650
whose description is such that I'll have to read it out, because it's fantastic.

4
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.750
An activist by vocation, a radio producer by education,

5
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:27.970
currently executive director of Mokrin House, a rural coworking and coliving space.

6
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.410
Tamara, please.

7
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:39.250
Thank you, Maja. There, someone is still taking their seat.

8
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.790
Hi everyone, my name is Tamara. Thank you, Maja, for that introduction.

9
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.670
I'm very glad to be here today and that you are here today.

10
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:55.050
My topic today will be much less technical than many we'll deal with today.

11
00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.670
It's more about something that, I think, has touched all of us to some extent this year —

12
00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.090
and that's how we work.

13
00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.410
All of us have some work habits, some work needs,

14
00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:10.650
and I think all of us this year faced some changes, each in our own way.

15
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.750
My story is maybe a tiny bit different.

16
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.870
Many people have had to work from home now,

17
00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:24.270
but I didn't have to, because I live at work.

18
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.010
The concept is rather strange, I wouldn't exactly recommend it to everyone.

19
00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:34.950
I moved from Dorćol to Mokrin, a village near Kikinda,

20
00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.390
to Mokrin House, the only coliving space in Serbia

21
00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.770
and one of the rare rural colivings in Europe.

22
00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:49.350
For those unfamiliar with the concept: coliving means people from all over the world come to us,

23
00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.570
live with us for three days, three weeks, three months,

24
00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:57.650
most often up to three months because of visas, on average a bit under three weeks,

25
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:03.510
and they live and work from there with us and become part of our community.

26
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:13.430
In our community there are basically two types of people who usually come — before COVID, of course.

27
00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.450
We have digital nomads, freelancers,

28
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:21.390
people who aren't tied to one physical place to do their work.

29
00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:26.830
And they come really from all over the world. When we were creating Mokrin House, we thought, like,

30
00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.450
it'll be some Europeans and it'll be people who write code,

31
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.210
because those are people who can do their work from anywhere,

32
00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.430
and Europeans because, like, then you're in touch with the team in a similar time zone.

33
00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.410
We missed on everything.

34
00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:46.750
From 2016 until this summer we've had really more than a thousand people,

35
00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:51.250
and they really weren't Europeans and really weren't only people who write code.

36
00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:57.450
We had poets, accountants, psychotherapists who work online.

37
00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:05.690
Really a mass of professions that we'd never have thought were so, like, location-independent.

38
00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.810
Anyway, the space itself is five houses on one plot,

39
00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.010
which serve different purposes.

40
00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.590
We have a workspace, this is the house that we see from this side.

41
00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:25.050
We have accommodation, one room that serves as a dining room,

42
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.250
there's a gym, a pool, bicycles.

43
00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:32.570
The point is for people to get out of their usual environment,

44
00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.290
to come and live this rural life for a while.

45
00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:41.570
But since we know how hard it is to move from an urban environment to a strictly rural one,

46
00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:47.190
we tried to somehow combine what, in our view, is best in these two worlds.

47
00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.470
We have fairly modern architecture, the fastest wifi in the village,

48
00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.070
which isn't exactly such an achievement,

49
00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.650
but it really is the fastest wifi in the village.

50
00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:02.770
We have all these conveniences of city life, and the surroundings are completely rural,

51
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:08.510
which means we have our own vegetable garden, and we know exactly, like, where the vegetables we eat come from,

52
00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:11.570
we raise our own chickens, ducks and geese

53
00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:16.390
and somehow let people feel confident in this rural experience,

54
00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:21.050
and yet still experience something new.

55
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:25.750
And people keep coming back, and when this whole situation started in March,

56
00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:29.630
many got in touch asking: listen, this is the best place in the world for quarantine,

57
00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:30.990
can we come to you?

58
00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:37.190
I said: it is. I mean, I, like, downloaded a pic in very poor resolution,

59
00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.030
which you're looking at now, it looks like a render, but I promise, it's real.

60
00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:50.230
I was alone at Mokrin House this spring and a good part of this summer too,

61
00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:54.390
because due to the safety situation we really couldn't allow people to be passing through there,

62
00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.810
and the borders were closed too,

63
00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.250
so there's no big crowd of digital nomads anymore,

64
00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:01.810
they've already found themselves some new bases.

65
00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:09.270
And this brought with it quite quirky challenges in how I work.

66
00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:14.830
Before that I'd already faced how hard it is to live at work,

67
00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:19.910
because, unlike most people, I don't have this: okay, I'm done,

68
00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.090
and leave the office and go home somewhere,

69
00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.930
because you see this door — I live behind this door,

70
00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:32.470
that's my room, and the next door, which you can't see, is our office.

71
00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:36.510
So technically I, like, leave the office, but actually I don't leave, I'm in the same building,

72
00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:40.590
and on top of that I live there, so it happens that, like, if there's some emergency,

73
00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.670
someone will call me at eleven, at two in the morning and so on.

74
00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:51.370
And this carries a lot of challenges with it, as you can imagine.

75
00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.450
I think many people have faced what it means to work from home

76
00:05:55.450 --> 00:06:00.770
and how hard it is to separate and say: okay, now I'm working, and now my workday is over,

77
00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:05.110
while you're in the same space. How hard it is to find that balance.

78
00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:09.910
And that is the topic that intrigued me even more

79
00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:15.370
during this whole process that, I want to say, we're still going through.

80
00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:20.330
And so, there's a phrase that's used quite often now,

81
00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:24.450
and I think that morning exercise is very beneficial, so let's all together

82
00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:29.030
roll our eyes, taking the opportunity: I say "new normal,"

83
00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.030
and everyone who needs to roll their eyes, go ahead.

84
00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:36.070
Because this year we really hear it very often.

85
00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:38.890
Does anyone know what the new normal is?

86
00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:43.790
Nobody knows what the new normal is, I don't know, nor...

87
00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:47.810
That is, we can imagine what it is, and we can consider that today it's something definite,

88
00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:52.190
but if we learned anything this year, it's that you can firmly

89
00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:55.410
believe in something, and then you need to be ready to

90
00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.550
change your mind. Right?

91
00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:03.750
And what's happening is this: I spent several months

92
00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:08.130
in a solo coliving, which is absurd enough in itself,

93
00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:13.030
and in parallel followed what's happening in our industry,

94
00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.810
and that's the coworking and coliving industry,

95
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.990
the industry of shared spaces for work, shared spaces for living,

96
00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.930
which in turn implies that human closeness is necessary,

97
00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:27.870
and physical contact, and being together in spaces,

98
00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.270
which we automatically had to write off, right?

99
00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:36.330
And then, little by little, this whole story began — what's happening on a global level.

100
00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.790
Coworking spaces suddenly with three times lower capacity,

101
00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:42.650
because distance must be kept.

102
00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:46.530
Events are being canceled, we no longer have conferences,

103
00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:48.870
no more meetings, no more exchange of any kind,

104
00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:53.470
and in parallel — what's happening with our productivity

105
00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:55.350
and what's happening with our creativity —

106
00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.450
those are the topics that interested me.

107
00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:01.410
And then I got an invitation to speak at this conference,

108
00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:04.750
I reread it three times to make sure it was really a chance to physically

109
00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.570
gather here. I can't describe how happy I am

110
00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:10.290
that we're here today, even though you're sitting here in masks,

111
00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.930
even though we have people watching via the stream.

112
00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:18.830
It's somehow incredible that we've finally gathered

113
00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:20.390
and can talk like this.

114
00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.610
My view is that conferences don't happen at the moment

115
00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:24.870
when I'm saying this now, a conference happens

116
00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.910
in all those breaks between, and before, and after,

117
00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:31.550
and in everything we'll talk about next,

118
00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.450
and in those Q&A sessions.

119
00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.430
I received an invitation to speak at this conference,

120
00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:40.050
and so, in that email there was one phrase,

121
00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:41.830
since I asked: okay, who are we addressing,

122
00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:44.390
what do we want to convey, what is our point,

123
00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:45.930
why are we gathering now,

124
00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.270
and from the organizers I got, among other things, the phrase:

125
00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.870
"in the hope that we'll help someone find a way

126
00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.630
to be productive in these strange times."

127
00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:56.890
And that made me think about

128
00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:00.470
how productive we really are in these strange times.

129
00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:04.450
And besides, it amuses me immensely

130
00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:06.950
how much we've started using "strange times,"

131
00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:08.870
like we've got some "uncertain times."

132
00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.210
"Good afternoon, Tamara, very nice to meet you,

133
00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.610
I hope you're doing well in these..."

134
00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:16.150
I looked in my inbox, and this is the first thing

135
00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:19.030
that I pulled out in the first minute of searching.

136
00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.390
"I hope your team in these unprecedented times..." —

137
00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.350
it's quite common. Then:

138
00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.890
"I hope you are healthy, well and in the flow with the circumstances."

139
00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:32.510
Nobody ever told me they hoped I was in the flow with the circumstances

140
00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:34.330
until the pandemic started.

141
00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:37.790
And "that you got through the previous period okay."

142
00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:41.950
Which in turn implies as if we think we've gone through some period,

143
00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:43.650
that we're done with something

144
00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:47.690
and that then, by that logic, this new normal already exists.

145
00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:51.870
My view, which I'd really like to discuss today, is

146
00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.890
that we haven't gone through this period,

147
00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:55.690
that this period is still going on

148
00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:59.850
and that we need to be aware at every moment

149
00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.670
that this is just one part of a phase of some change

150
00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:04.510
that doesn't come so quickly.

151
00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.650
The question was how to find a way

152
00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:11.890
to be productive in these strange times.

153
00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:15.370
I immediately remembered one wonderful study —

154
00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:18.950
for all of us nerds who love reading studies and scientific papers so much.

155
00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:26.070
Stanford professor Nicholas Bloom conducted a quite serious analysis,

156
00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.650
they used a Chinese company called Ctrip.

157
00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:32.490
It's, I think, the largest Chinese travel agency,

158
00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:35.090
with 16,000 employees

159
00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:37.350
and which had two problems they wanted to solve.

160
00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:39.970
One problem was that they have an office in Shanghai,

161
00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.530
where the rent is terribly high,

162
00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:44.890
and that became a very serious expense for them.

163
00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.270
And their second problem was that

164
00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.550
they had incredible staff turnover.

165
00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:57.730
About 50% of employees were changing — that kind of turnover every year.

166
00:10:57.910 --> 00:10:59.490
And that's — I don't know if there's anyone from HR here —

167
00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:02.010
but that's about the biggest nightmare

168
00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.070
you can imagine:

169
00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:06.170
constantly hiring and onboarding people

170
00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:09.410
and constantly working with such a large team.

171
00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:15.750
And then they basically took one division of this company

172
00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:17.370
and said: let's talk about

173
00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:18.590
whether it's possible to work from home.

174
00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:19.670
The first thought was clear:

175
00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.990
it means people will slack off at home

176
00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:23.890
if we don't have constant supervision.

177
00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.890
And the specific thing was

178
00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:28.670
that these were people who work in a call center.

179
00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:31.490
That's very important, because not everyone could

180
00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:32.910
just work from home like that.

181
00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:36.830
The arrangement was this: they took... in this division

182
00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.530
about a thousand employees.

183
00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:42.170
508 met a certain criterion.

184
00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:44.290
Among other things — that they had worked at least six months

185
00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:45.990
at this company, to even have the option

186
00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.050
of switching to working from home,

187
00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:48.610
which means they had to some extent

188
00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.010
integrated into the team.

189
00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:54.230
And so out of these thousand people

190
00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:57.630
508 met these criteria,

191
00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:00.770
about half ended up getting this experience —

192
00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:03.670
nine months working four days a week from home.

193
00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.130
And it was pretty random.

194
00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:07.450
That is, 508 people wanted to,

195
00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:09.470
and then they said: everyone who was born

196
00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:11.830
on an even day will work from home,

197
00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:13.390
and the odd ones will stay in the office.

198
00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:15.550
Well, nothing special happened

199
00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:17.410
with those who worked in the office.

200
00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:22.230
They really stayed loyal,

201
00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.290
worked, had the same results,

202
00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:25.530
but what's interesting is what happened

203
00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:26.830
with the team that went home.

204
00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:31.890
Their productivity jumped by 13%.

205
00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:33.930
Of course, how is productivity measured?

206
00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:35.550
If you're in a call center it's much easier

207
00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.750
than if you do some slightly more abstract work.

208
00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:41.730
Among other things, in these studies,

209
00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:43.230
when people actually gave feedback,

210
00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.790
they said they were less distracted.

211
00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:47.470
Because when you're in a call center

212
00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:48.870
and everyone around you is talking,

213
00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:50.070
there's a constant noise

214
00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:51.270
and it's hard to concentrate.

215
00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:54.010
On top of that, over these nine months

216
00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:55.910
people took sick leave less often.

217
00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:57.830
Which can also be linked to the fact

218
00:12:57.830 --> 00:12:59.730
that when you're in contact with fewer people —

219
00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:01.290
if we learned anything this year —

220
00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:03.230
there's less risk that you'll catch

221
00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.830
some bug, illness, virus.

222
00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:10.130
Of course, the firm was thrilled.

223
00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:11.770
They immediately said:

224
00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:14.090
this is great, we want to continue it,

225
00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:16.190
and said: the whole firm can

226
00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.250
now go work from home.

227
00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:20.170
They prepared protocols,

228
00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:22.930
thought through how it would happen,

229
00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.250
and saw that people were happy.

230
00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:30.690
Of the people who worked from home,

231
00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.050
who really had high productivity,

232
00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:35.270
who were happy, satisfied,

233
00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:36.270
wonderful results —

234
00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:37.570
I really warmly recommend

235
00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:38.530
reading this study.

236
00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.610
We can learn a lot from it.

237
00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:44.950
Half of the people ended up going back to the office after all.

238
00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:47.970
They had the option to keep working that way forever.

239
00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.710
Now, I suppose, most of us can understand

240
00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:53.070
what actually happened.

241
00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:56.430
Their main reason for going back to the office

242
00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:57.930
was loneliness.

243
00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.540
I think loneliness

244
00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:07.060
is something that at some point this year hit all of us.

245
00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:10.000
Even those of us who live alone,

246
00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:11.940
those who live with families,

247
00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.480
those who live with their partners —

248
00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.840
loneliness again,

249
00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:19.360
just like we have an invisible enemy,

250
00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:21.120
it's also invisible.

251
00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.940
We have Zoom, I have FaceTime.

252
00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:24.380
At one point I had

253
00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:26.380
ten and a half hours of screen time on my phone.

254
00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:29.520
That is, we were in touch with each other, right?

255
00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.760
But loneliness is exactly what kept tormenting us.

256
00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.520
What's interesting in connection with this study —

257
00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:39.080
it was done in March 2013,

258
00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.700
which is also a very important factor,

259
00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:44.700
considering that this is a moment

260
00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.620
when a big company, —

261
00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:50.260
and one of the people who signed this study —

262
00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:52.220
is a co-founder of this company.

263
00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:54.640
That is, they had fully prepared

264
00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.400
the whole protocol and the way

265
00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.860
they'd continue working from home.

266
00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:02.100
They have an idea of the management mindset,

267
00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:04.260
they have an idea of what the focus is:

268
00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:06.620
cost reduction, I mentioned,

269
00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:12.440
and also reducing this staff turnover.

270
00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:13.900
That is, they cooperated fully,

271
00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.720
prepared, worked on it for nine months

272
00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:17.440
and in the end got fantastic results —

273
00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.740
for people to say: listen, I'd still like to go back to the office.

274
00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.560
Just when all this started this year,

275
00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:27.380
many referred to Nicholas Bloom,

276
00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.400
who really is, like, an expert in this field,

277
00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.260
and it's very interesting why he said then:

278
00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:33.500
I know that after this huge study

279
00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.580
that we conducted, you all want me to say now:

280
00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:37.940
working from home is what will save us,

281
00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:39.280
it's the best thing in the world,

282
00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.020
give up offices, we can all work from home,

283
00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:44.580
productivity grows by 13%.

284
00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:46.840
It even turned out that for those people who continued

285
00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:48.580
working from home at Ctrip,

286
00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.260
productivity grew by 22%.

287
00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:52.880
Everything is fantastic,

288
00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:54.160
but Nicholas Bloom said:

289
00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:55.840
listen, but, people, I have four kids,

290
00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:57.980
and I live with my wife,

291
00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:00.120
and I have a four-year-old

292
00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:02.000
who comes into my study,

293
00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:03.160
which isn't a study,

294
00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:06.300
I have people who work with me,

295
00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:08.920
who share a house with roommates,

296
00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.720
and they only have a dining table to work at,

297
00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:13.640
and they can't even take calls,

298
00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:15.720
also we have people who don't have

299
00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:17.520
a separate room for an office,

300
00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:18.960
there are people who are suffering,

301
00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:21.500
who find it very hard to distance themselves,

302
00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:23.220
there are people who are online all the time

303
00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.640
and effectively at work all the time.

304
00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:27.720
And then I turned to what's

305
00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.640
happening right now, in 2020,

306
00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:31.660
using Germany as an example.

307
00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:32.700
I really love Germany,

308
00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:33.720
because it's a great example:

309
00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.020
we have 42 million working people,

310
00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:40.740
we have a fairly analytical society,

311
00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:44.380
and they really did conduct a number of studies.

312
00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.800
They came to very interesting results.

313
00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:50.380
The first — what we see:

314
00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:52.520
people said that working from home

315
00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:54.300
has no disadvantages.

316
00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:56.660
90% of people said that working from home

317
00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:57.800
has no disadvantages.

318
00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:01.640
Then a whole controversy began

319
00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:04.720
around the legal regulation of working from home

320
00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:07.340
and whether it's a right that every worker

321
00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:09.040
can demand and can use.

322
00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:12.640
60% of people fought and said they want

323
00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:15.440
it to become, essentially, their basic right

324
00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:16.860
to work — working from home.

325
00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:18.680
Of course, we're talking only about people

326
00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:20.120
who can work from home.

327
00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:25.040
Unfortunately that's around 40% of people,

328
00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:27.600
it's considered that maybe they can work from home,

329
00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.240
that is, not work from an office

330
00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:30.520
or in the field.

331
00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:34.980
42% of people wanted to continue

332
00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:36.780
working from home after the pandemic too.

333
00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:39.620
In this study they talked

334
00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.280
to the 500 largest companies in Germany

335
00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:43.460
and came to these results,

336
00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:45.040
and they're all quite positive.

337
00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:45.380
Right?

338
00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.720
You must agree, if 90% of people say

339
00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:49.180
that working from home has no disadvantages

340
00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:52.140
and that they want to keep working this way

341
00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:52.880
in the future too.

342
00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:54.520
That tells us that it is, essentially,

343
00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:55.660
a great option.

344
00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:00.660
My question is — how great an option is it.

345
00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:01.880
You'll notice that I won't

346
00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:03.080
give you many answers today.

347
00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:04.800
I think it would be somehow too

348
00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:07.540
pretentious to come and lay out here

349
00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:08.640
some great truths.

350
00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:11.180
The key difference

351
00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:12.760
between the 2013 study

352
00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:15.400
and the 2020 studies

353
00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:17.780
is that we're talking about a global crisis.

354
00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:20.000
We're talking about a global pandemic,

355
00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:21.000
about the fact that we receive

356
00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:23.660
a countless amount of information every day,

357
00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:26.460
which completely contradicts each other.

358
00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:29.280
That on one side we have panic,

359
00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:30.520
great uncertainty.

360
00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:34.560
There was also another study

361
00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.580
which showed that before the pandemic started

362
00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:39.580
8% of workers in Germany

363
00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:41.820
worked outside the office.

364
00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:42.980
That is, from home,

365
00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:44.480
but could work from anywhere.

366
00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.480
And that this number jumped to 35.

367
00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:48.060
According to another study — 42.

368
00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:50.520
But in any case it was truly a huge jump.

369
00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.540
Now there's one big question.

370
00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:55.740
If someone tells you now:

371
00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:57.880
hey, do you want to keep working from home —

372
00:18:58.060 --> 00:18:59.660
and you have a child at home

373
00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:02.880
who is maybe studying online,

374
00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:04.720
or you're afraid

375
00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:05.780
to go back to the office,

376
00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:08.000
because you have some small office

377
00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:09.520
with a lot of people or something else —

378
00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:11.460
the question arises:

379
00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:13.420
did the person say they want to keep working

380
00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:15.300
from home

381
00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.180
because they love working from home

382
00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:18.960
or out of some fear.

383
00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:20.400
And here's what I'd like:

384
00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:22.800
if there's one thing we think about today —

385
00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:25.260
it's to realize

386
00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:27.740
that we're still in the process

387
00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:29.540
of finding out what awaits us,

388
00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:30.520
and that we still don't know

389
00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:31.740
even what stage we're at,

390
00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:34.660
and that only when it's over will we be able to understand where we are.

391
00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:37.260
Work-life balance is also

392
00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:38.880
another one of those phrases

393
00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.200
that I like to roll my eyes at,

394
00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.020
like daily cardio,

395
00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.280
because we're all witnesses to the fact

396
00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:46.720
that there actually isn't a separate

397
00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:49.080
life and separate work.

398
00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:51.140
Right? Work is still part of life.

399
00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:54.360
And I think all of us have now experienced

400
00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:56.460
how really hard it is

401
00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:58.020
to balance that,

402
00:19:58.020 --> 00:19:58.980
and to even figure out

403
00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:00.500
at what point work ends

404
00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:02.240
and life begins,

405
00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:04.820
or life ends and work begins,

406
00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:05.660
which is even worse.

407
00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:07.840
I think all of us —

408
00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:10.040
maybe someone didn't,

409
00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:11.080
but if someone didn't,

410
00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:12.280
please come up to me afterwards

411
00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:13.480
and tell me how you managed —

412
00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:16.780
faced many new challenges

413
00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:18.980
that concerned setting up this barrier

414
00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:19.720
and separation.

415
00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:20.560
Some are nodding their heads,

416
00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:21.060
thank you.

417
00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:25.080
This is something that is perhaps

418
00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:26.460
part of our new normal.

419
00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:27.980
This is something that perhaps

420
00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:29.260
still awaits us.

421
00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:30.440
So, I've shown you

422
00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:33.260
roughly what this big study in China is,

423
00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:34.760
which is a really extensive study.

424
00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:36.080
Some of the information

425
00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:37.180
that we got from Germany —

426
00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:39.200
there's really a huge amount here,

427
00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:41.880
firms conducted their own internal analyses,

428
00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:44.580
in different countries some institutes,

429
00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.360
universities were doing this.

430
00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:49.360
What I'd like

431
00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:51.600
us to discuss today too

432
00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.340
is the case of this room right now.

433
00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.320
My view is that

434
00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:03.060
somehow in our society,

435
00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:05.620
especially in the business environment,

436
00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:07.700
vulnerability is something we don't practice

437
00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:08.240
enough.

438
00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.160
During quarantine I was terribly lonely.

439
00:21:12.020 --> 00:21:13.660
I wonder whether anyone here has experienced

440
00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:15.500
loneliness

441
00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:16.900
over the past six months.

442
00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:22.060
Yes, thank you.

443
00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:25.100
Has anyone here had

444
00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.480
a moment of complete

445
00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:28.520
"I can't take it anymore,"

446
00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:30.600
and at least one moment

447
00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.420
when nothing made sense?

448
00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:38.220
Understood, yes.

449
00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:40.000
Yes, absolutely.

450
00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:43.020
Has anyone here had

451
00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:45.120
thoughts along the lines of:

452
00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:46.300
hey, I don't want to do this anymore.

453
00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:47.760
I won't do this job anymore

454
00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:48.740
when this is over.

455
00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:49.520
There are some like that too.

456
00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.040
Fewer such cases, yes.

457
00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:53.860
I think all of us have faced

458
00:21:53.860 --> 00:21:55.300
both the fleetingness of life,

459
00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:57.560
and the fragility of human life too,

460
00:21:57.560 --> 00:21:58.980
and this additionally pushed us

461
00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:00.040
to think about where

462
00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:02.180
we want to be in the future.

463
00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:05.360
I'll turn now to look —

464
00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:07.320
there, Viktor writes:

465
00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:08.460
what's happening today isn't working from home,

466
00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:09.320
it's torture.

467
00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:11.080
I've been working from home for the past three years,

468
00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:12.480
and this year the kids aren't going to kindergarten.

469
00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:13.460
Absolutely.

470
00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:14.800
I think that's what

471
00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:15.940
we need to think about.

472
00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:21.600
This is working from home

473
00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:22.440
during a pandemic,

474
00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:23.160
this is working from home

475
00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:23.840
while fighting

476
00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:26.200
an invisible enemy.

477
00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:28.040
Would you go back

478
00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:28.860
to working in an office,

479
00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:29.420
and what do you think

480
00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:30.760
is the biggest drawback of working from home?

481
00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:33.600
When I used to work from home,

482
00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:35.400
meaning when I really had, like, a home

483
00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.680
where I work, and wasn't living at work,

484
00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:40.180
my big problem was

485
00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:43.840
that I used the same laptop for work,

486
00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:47.300
for classes on Skype

487
00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:49.060
and also for Netflix,

488
00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:51.180
and that somehow all the time

489
00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:52.620
reminded me of work,

490
00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:53.400
and all the time, like,

491
00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:54.440
there pops up, like,

492
00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:55.980
a ping, some email and all that.

493
00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:57.340
My big problem was

494
00:22:57.340 --> 00:22:58.680
that I couldn't separate it,

495
00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:00.080
because I used one email

496
00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:01.880
for a bunch of different projects,

497
00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:03.440
both personal and work and the like,

498
00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:04.560
and I couldn't

499
00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:05.600
distance myself from it.

500
00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:07.140
What I did then:

501
00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:09.440
when I go to work,

502
00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:11.160
even though I work in the very same room,

503
00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:13.280
I would put on shoes,

504
00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:15.140
even though I wasn't going anywhere,

505
00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:16.720
and for me it was like:

506
00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:19.000
hey, I'm off to work,

507
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:19.640
adios,

508
00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:21.460
and, like, I sit down like a businesswoman,

509
00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:22.440
that is, no pajamas,

510
00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:23.900
I'm wearing, like, shoes,

511
00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:25.820
I'm fully dressed for work

512
00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:26.900
and then I do that,

513
00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:27.880
and then I, like,

514
00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:29.300
take off my shoes, change into pajamas,

515
00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:30.460
and then it's a completely different vibe.

516
00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:31.720
And it wasn't like —

517
00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:32.600
I can't work

518
00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:33.500
if I'm in pajamas,

519
00:23:33.500 --> 00:23:36.280
and I can't really laze around

520
00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.280
if I'm in shoes:

521
00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:39.820
this is work time.

522
00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:43.720
What else

523
00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:44.520
is a big drawback,

524
00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:45.900
I think, is

525
00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:48.800
precisely that,

526
00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:50.660
when people work

527
00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:51.940
from home in general,

528
00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:53.420
it wasn't something

529
00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:54.440
that all of us had

530
00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:55.320
as an experience.

531
00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:56.300
And the big advantage

532
00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:57.000
of all this,

533
